1
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:04,312
Speaker: In January of 1999, security
officers at the most secretive

2
00:00:04,312 --> 00:00:09,662
intelligence agency in the United States
pulled a small, fuzzy, wide-eyed toy off

3
00:00:09,662 --> 00:00:14,991
an employee's desk and confiscated it
like it was a listening device, because

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,261
that is exactly what they thought it was.

5
00:00:17,812 --> 00:00:20,412
The agency was the
National Security Agency.

6
00:00:20,982 --> 00:00:22,491
The toy was a Furby.

7
00:00:23,242 --> 00:00:27,562
And for a few strange weeks, one of the
most powerful surveillance organizations

8
00:00:27,562 --> 00:00:31,702
on Earth was more afraid of a thirty-five
dollar Christmas present than almost

9
00:00:31,712 --> 00:00:33,461
anything else on its own property.

10
00:00:34,302 --> 00:00:38,611
Here is the question worth asking: not
whether a talking toy could actually

11
00:00:38,611 --> 00:00:40,772
spy on the National Security Agency.

12
00:00:41,561 --> 00:00:45,451
It could not, and within
days, even the agency's own

13
00:00:45,451 --> 00:00:46,922
officials seemed to sense that.

14
00:00:47,712 --> 00:00:51,532
The real question is how a plastic
and fake fur owl convinced grown

15
00:00:51,532 --> 00:00:54,932
adults trained to detect real
threats that it might be listening.

16
00:00:55,831 --> 00:00:59,911
That question has very little to
do with spycraft and everything

17
00:00:59,912 --> 00:01:03,511
to do with how well this toy
was built to fake being alive.

18
00:01:04,491 --> 00:01:08,151
The story starts two years
earlier with two inventors named

19
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:10,111
Dave Hampton and Caleb Chung.

20
00:01:11,072 --> 00:01:15,051
Hampton had spent years building
animatronics and novelty electronics,

21
00:01:15,792 --> 00:01:20,042
the kind of small mechanical creatures
that move and react just convincingly

22
00:01:20,042 --> 00:01:21,782
enough to surprise someone holding them.

23
00:01:22,851 --> 00:01:27,731
Chung brought a background in puppetry and
film special effects, the discipline of

24
00:01:27,731 --> 00:01:31,961
making something lifeless read as alive
through nothing but timing and motion.

25
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:37,251
Together, the two of them spent nine
months designing the toy on paper and

26
00:01:37,252 --> 00:01:42,111
another nine months actually building
it, chasing one very specific goal.

27
00:01:43,072 --> 00:01:45,141
They did not just want a toy that talked.

28
00:01:45,911 --> 00:01:49,121
They wanted a toy that felt like it
was discovering the world in real

29
00:01:49,121 --> 00:01:53,461
time, right in front of the person
holding it, blinking and reacting a

30
00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:59,124
half second after you did something,
the same way a real animal would They

31
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:03,164
partnered with a toy inventor named
Richard Levy, who brought the concept

32
00:02:03,164 --> 00:02:05,304
to a company called Tiger Electronics.

33
00:02:06,284 --> 00:02:10,914
Tiger's leader, Roger Shiffman, bought
the rights and unveiled the toy at the

34
00:02:10,914 --> 00:02:14,953
American International Toy Fair in New
York in early nineteen ninety-eight.

35
00:02:15,654 --> 00:02:18,334
The response from buyers
in the room was immediate.

36
00:02:19,013 --> 00:02:23,164
This was not a normal-looking gadget on
a shelf full of normal-looking gadgets.

37
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:29,383
It had huge blinking eyes, oversized
ears that swiveled and folded, and a

38
00:02:29,384 --> 00:02:33,954
beak that opened and closed every time
it spoke its own invented language.

39
00:02:34,793 --> 00:02:38,144
Buyers who had seen thousands of
toy pitches over their careers

40
00:02:38,374 --> 00:02:41,703
reportedly stopped to watch this
one longer than they meant to.

41
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:46,153
The toy launched to the public on
October second, nineteen ninety-eight,

42
00:02:46,594 --> 00:02:48,713
priced at around thirty-five dollars.

43
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:53,263
And then something happened that Tiger
Electronics had not fully prepared

44
00:02:53,263 --> 00:02:57,383
for, despite the strong reaction
at the toy fair months earlier.

45
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:03,124
Demand did not just meet expectations,
it broke them completely, the same way a

46
00:03:03,124 --> 00:03:07,563
handful of other toy crazes had blindsided
manufacturers earlier that same decade.

47
00:03:08,523 --> 00:03:12,283
In its first partial year on
shelves, the company sold one

48
00:03:12,283 --> 00:03:13,753
point eight million units.

49
00:03:14,453 --> 00:03:18,103
By the following year, that
number jumped to fourteen million.

50
00:03:18,943 --> 00:03:22,673
Over its first three years of
production, more than forty million

51
00:03:22,673 --> 00:03:24,663
of these toys sold worldwide.

52
00:03:25,493 --> 00:03:28,433
During the nineteen ninety-eight
holiday season specifically,

53
00:03:28,894 --> 00:03:31,873
parents could not find them
anywhere near the original price.

54
00:03:32,754 --> 00:03:35,254
Stores limited customers to one per visit.

55
00:03:35,923 --> 00:03:40,113
Local news programs ran segments on which
stores had a truck arriving that morning.

56
00:03:40,913 --> 00:03:44,904
Resale prices climbed past one hundred
dollars, and in the most desperate

57
00:03:44,904 --> 00:03:48,643
cases, buyers reported paying
several hundred dollars to secondhand

58
00:03:48,644 --> 00:03:52,213
sellers just to have one sitting
under the tree on Christmas morning.

59
00:03:53,203 --> 00:03:58,573
Part of what made the toy so addictive for
kids and adults alike was its core trick.

60
00:03:59,638 --> 00:04:04,178
Out of the box, it spoke almost
entirely in its own made-up language, a

61
00:04:04,178 --> 00:04:08,697
string of nonsense syllables somewhere
between baby talk and a foreign tongue.

62
00:04:09,678 --> 00:04:14,208
Owners actually began compiling informal
dictionaries of these words, trading

63
00:04:14,208 --> 00:04:17,568
translations with each other the way
people once traded notes on a foreign

64
00:04:17,568 --> 00:04:19,338
language learned on a family trip.

65
00:04:20,237 --> 00:04:24,448
But over time, as a child
interacted with it, English words

66
00:04:24,457 --> 00:04:28,267
gradually started replacing the
gibberish one phrase at a time.

67
00:04:29,117 --> 00:04:33,308
It genuinely seemed to be learning
how to talk the same way a small child

68
00:04:33,317 --> 00:04:38,417
does: listening, absorbing, and slowly
getting better at speaking back.

69
00:04:39,307 --> 00:04:43,688
Owners swore their own toy had a
personality, a mood, even a sense

70
00:04:43,688 --> 00:04:47,327
of humor, despite every unit on
the assembly line running the

71
00:04:47,328 --> 00:04:49,378
identical program underneath the fur.

72
00:04:50,197 --> 00:04:52,607
That illusion was the entire product.

73
00:04:53,237 --> 00:04:57,597
It was clever, it was effective,
and it was about to cause a problem

74
00:04:57,598 --> 00:04:59,537
nobody at the toy company saw coming.

75
00:05:00,258 --> 00:05:06,608
By January of 1999, just over three
months after launch, that same illusion

76
00:05:06,617 --> 00:05:11,258
reached the National Security Agency
headquartered in Fort Meade, Maryland.

77
00:05:12,087 --> 00:05:16,167
On January 13th, the agency
issued an order banning the toys

78
00:05:16,168 --> 00:05:17,677
from its buildings entirely.

79
00:05:18,547 --> 00:05:21,797
The Navy's shipyard in Portsmouth,
Virginia, followed with a

80
00:05:21,807 --> 00:05:23,447
similar restriction of its own.

81
00:05:24,507 --> 00:05:28,288
Separately, the Federal Aviation
Administration weighed in as well,

82
00:05:28,717 --> 00:05:32,347
treating the toy the same way it treated
any other battery-powered electronic

83
00:05:32,348 --> 00:05:36,827
device, something that had to be switched
off during takeoff and landing rather

84
00:05:36,827 --> 00:05:38,737
than left running in a passenger's bag.

85
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:42,627
Three different arms of the federal
government, in other words, all

86
00:05:42,627 --> 00:05:46,967
decided independently that this
specific toy deserved a written policy.
